WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.914
Who watches The Watcher? We did. Here's where the&nbsp;
characters ended up in Ryan Murphy's newest show.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.700
In an era of Ring doorbells and CCTV cameras&nbsp;
and Siri and Alexa, the idea that we're under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.040
almost constant surveillance is prime material for&nbsp;
suspense, or even horror. "The Watcher" takes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.200
one step further by giving this sense of being&nbsp;
watched a physical form. The sometimes childlike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.340
often poetic writing of the Watcher only&nbsp;
contributes to its dreamy spookiness.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.640
The story starts with the Brannock&nbsp;
family being watched not only by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.200
their neighbors and social betters, but by&nbsp;
a faceless spirit who is sometimes loving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.120
and sometimes hateful and frightening. As&nbsp;
the series continues from episode to episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.640
the list of suspects changes and mutates, each&nbsp;
episode ending with a new potential culprit.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.680
It's reminiscent of the original "Pretty Little&nbsp;
Liars," which almost always ended episodes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.360
a certainty that so-and-so was the villain,&nbsp;
only to have that certainty blown away in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.560
the first minutes of the next episode. It's&nbsp;
a powerful, smart setup that leads you on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.528
making it utterly effective for a&nbsp;
show constructed for a binge watch.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.480
By the end of things, the Brannocks'&nbsp;
marriage is in tatters, even if their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.140
relationship with their children seems&nbsp;
mended. They are in a social free fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.420
and the financial power dynamic has flipped. From&nbsp;
the first moment, so much of Dean's obsession with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.040
the house is about his need to prove himself&nbsp;
as a provider — which is to say, as a man.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.140
Dean's toxicity is broadcast early and&nbsp;
often, with his obsessive focus on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.460
daughter's teenage body and love life,&nbsp;
as well as his tendency toward violence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.260
It doesn't take terribly long for his&nbsp;
façade to crumble and fall apart as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.340
pressure of being under the Watcher's eye&nbsp;
makes his self-consciousness even worse.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.260
In the end, Dean has become a second Watcher,&nbsp;
surveilling and perhaps even writing to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.000
house's newest tenants. Despite Theodora and&nbsp;
Nora's best efforts to help Dean get free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.020
he is unable to pry his sense of self&nbsp;
away from the house and we're left with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.196
the feeling that Dean is going to be consumed&nbsp;
by the house just like the Watchers before him.

00:01:56.331 --> 00:01:57.418
"Beautiful house."

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.380
"Thanks. Just moved in."

00:02:01.514 --> 00:02:03.960
Nora Brannock is a social climber from the start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.860
willing to take her orders from the&nbsp;
bizarre but socially comfortable realtor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.940
Karen Calhoun when it comes to making&nbsp;
her way in this new suburban paradise.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:17.460
This proclivity is softened somewhat by the fact&nbsp;
that her accomplishments as a ceramicist have made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.960
her a respected member of the art world.&nbsp;
Art is a world of access and connections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.860
and that's all Nora's really looking for&nbsp;
when they arrive in Westfield. By the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.180
of course, she's terrorizing her realtor by&nbsp;
pretending to be the Watcher in her old house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.520
but that's a long way from where she starts.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.440
Nora ends the series on a high note — her&nbsp;
gallery pieces are selling, and she's supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.060
the family. While she gives credit to Dean for&nbsp;
visiting his counselor, she also doesn't trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.800
him and follows him on his house-watching errands.&nbsp;
Whatever the answers to the many mysteries they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.310
uncovered, we know that she's at least willing to&nbsp;
lie to him and definitely aware of her options.

00:02:51.548 --> 00:02:55.860
Dakota ends the series by leaving his calling&nbsp;
card in the mailbox of the new family living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.600
in the house, as if to signal that there is&nbsp;
a cycle starting up again. While Dakota is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.320
acquitted of any wrongdoing — first by&nbsp;
the laws of New Jersey and then by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.540
own dogged work — the circumstances leave us&nbsp;
uneasy. In a story about total surveillance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.260
Dakota is the living embodiment of the&nbsp;
concept. Perhaps that's the meaning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.660
his reappearance at the house&nbsp;
in the series' final moments.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.740
Ellie somehow manages to find peace with&nbsp;
her parents after the struggle and terror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.640
of their situation calms down. She won't&nbsp;
exactly take an active role in the family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.540
but she seems to have proven something&nbsp;
to Dean by the end. He spends much less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.240
time worrying about her love life&nbsp;
than we see throughout the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.340
although he still heckles her about lipstick and&nbsp;
bare shoulders. Nonetheless, it would be nice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.737
think that Ellie is strengthened or emboldened&nbsp;
in some way by the family's experiences.

00:03:39.737 --> 00:03:44.520
One of the show's most unbelievable twists&nbsp;
involves Mitch and Mo, the Brannocks' neighbors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.120
across the street. They are apparently&nbsp;
wheeled out of their house on gurneys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.860
dead from a shotgun murder-suicide, only&nbsp;
to miraculously reappear a few episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.780
later. As it turns out, they were merely on&nbsp;
a vacation; the dead couple were actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.040
pair of unhoused people brought to the house&nbsp;
and killed by Mo and Mitch's son Christopher.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.020
Maureen, or "Big Mo," ends the series with a&nbsp;
lot more dignity than we might expect. Mitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.640
dies of an embolism offscreen; Christopher is&nbsp;
locked up probably forever for his murders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.260
and Mo herself is dying of cancer. But&nbsp;
she is vindicated of any involvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.800
with the Watcher or any of the other&nbsp;
mysterious goings-on in the neighborhood.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.820
One of the most compelling dangling threads is&nbsp;
exactly what Christopher was thinking when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.140
lured the couple to his parents' empty home&nbsp;
and then staged their deaths. Maureen offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.640
no real answers on this count and just seems&nbsp;
exhausted by the whole thing in the end. Perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.040
a life cut senselessly short by illness has&nbsp;
prepared her for senselessness in other arenas.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.700
Pearl and Jasper aren't quite in the mix to&nbsp;
the degree that you might assume throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.360
the season, but they seem to have something&nbsp;
to do with the tunnel system and living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.320
space underground, as well as with the&nbsp;
mysterious man known as William Webster.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.120
Jasper ends the season chopping wood in a&nbsp;
menacing way on the property, as if to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.685
the necessary creepy ambience for the new family&nbsp;
to understand what they've gotten themselves into.

00:04:58.685 --> 00:05:02.280
“A baby skeleton has 100 more&nbsp;
bones than an adult skeleton."

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.180
However, Pearl ends the story as&nbsp;
she began it — running the Westfield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.769
Preservation Society and working&nbsp;
toward its ends, no matter the cost.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.740
The newest member of the&nbsp;
Westfield Preservation Society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:17.940
and seemingly Pearl's henchman, is an&nbsp;
enigma. First, he presents himself at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.260
the open house as "John" and discusses his&nbsp;
daughter and a nearby church at length.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.380
Then we learn of a man named John&nbsp;
Graff who attended the same church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.300
lived in the house and — with shades of "The&nbsp;
Amityville Horror" — murdered his entire family.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.340
Dean is obsessed with the idea that his strange&nbsp;
visitor John is actually John Graff hanging around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.380
the site of his massacre and possibly leaving&nbsp;
little clues for Dean to find. This avenue of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.580
exploration is curtailed because by the end,&nbsp;
John Graff — or, as he introduces himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.900
William "Bill" Webster — seems like one of the&nbsp;
likeliest candidates for the Watcher himself.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.340
He works with Pearl to scare the Brannocks&nbsp;
away, sure, but he often just stands around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.000
the house with no sign of how he got&nbsp;
there. For a series that takes its joy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.900
in the unraveling of its characters, we don't&nbsp;
see much of his real motives or his real self,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.531
at all. He's a wildcard in a&nbsp;
way the other suspects are not.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.320
Andrew is an unreliable narrator who tells stories&nbsp;
that make very little sense and conceals the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.760
that he's a professional actor, so Dean can&nbsp;
be forgiven for mistrusting him. And while we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.080
get a momentary sense of relief when Theodora&nbsp;
admits on her deathbed to manipulating Andrew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:24.060
of course we're meant to understand that nothing&nbsp;
Theodora says in that conversation is true.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.320
We can assume that Andrew was driven&nbsp;
somewhat mad by the house, and its Watcher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.120
just as we see happen to Dean. Andrew is the&nbsp;
only one who knows about the tunnels at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.380
and it's clear he has a troubled past with&nbsp;
Mitch and Maureen. We end the season with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.820
Andrew sobbing and staring at the house, which&nbsp;
doesn't seem like a great sign for his well-being.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:45.300
But he isn't the Watcher, even&nbsp;
if it seems this final tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.780
appearance outside the house might have&nbsp;
greater meaning. Andrew is frustrating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.380
because he believes in nonsense but he&nbsp;
is also willing to be more honest than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.014
some of the other characters are with&nbsp;
Dean regarding his warped perceptions.

00:06:56.394 --> 00:06:59.940
Karen Calhoun is a real estate&nbsp;
agent in the grandest sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.460
always working up deals and looking for angles.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.571
"You should have it. And you&nbsp;
know why? Cause you're rich."

00:07:07.571 --> 00:07:12.780
While Karen's storyline gets a bit mangled by&nbsp;
the end, her arc is easy to read: She starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.400
off mercenary but kind, tries everything she&nbsp;
can to get Nora to behave herself socially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.320
and mostly seems to be along for the ride. But&nbsp;
in the end, she's so twisted up by her dealings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.700
with the rapidly deteriorating Brannocks that&nbsp;
she becomes sort of dazzlingly horrible when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:31.020
she buys the house herself and finds it just&nbsp;
as haunted as everyone else who moves in.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.920
Karen's an interesting case because by&nbsp;
the end, we can see the whole story from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:39.360
her perspective. She has every right to be&nbsp;
exasperated and done with the whole family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.760
and angry with Nora specifically. Of course, being&nbsp;
right will not save you and when we last see her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:48.278
Karen is running down Boulevard&nbsp;
in her dressing gown, screaming.

00:07:48.278 --> 00:07:51.300
Roger is the inspiration for&nbsp;
the Watcher in many ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.980
and just as creepy as he seems. For starters,&nbsp;
his wife was a high school student of his,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:01.560
and he has a hair-trigger anger problem. But&nbsp;
is he the Watcher? The jury says no. He's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.820
unbalanced groomer, but his association&nbsp;
with the house is purely as an admirer.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.900
Again and again, from literally the first&nbsp;
scene of the show, we see people dazzled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.620
and overwhelmed by the house's beauty.&nbsp;
There is something transcendent about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.000
the house itself that we cannot see, and&nbsp;
it controls everyone who comes across it.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.540
Roger Kaplan is the best example,&nbsp;
because he literally created his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.960
approach to teaching around his love&nbsp;
of houses — he is famous for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.516
lesson in which his students write "love&nbsp;
letters" to a house somewhere in town.

00:08:28.516 --> 00:08:35.733
"Your next assignment is to find a house that&nbsp;
you love. And to write a love letter to it."

00:08:35.733 --> 00:08:40.380
The Watcher takes this on as a sacred duty,&nbsp;
in their own way, and in the end it's just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:43.993
funny complication that Roger remains as&nbsp;
enamored with the house as anyone else.

00:08:43.993 --> 00:08:45.905
"Your house knows me, your house likes me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.905 --> 00:08:49.055
your house wishes that I was&nbsp;
living in it instead of you."

00:08:49.055 --> 00:08:53.040
Theodora Birch turns the season finale&nbsp;
inside out with her admission that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.100
is a previous owner of the house&nbsp;
and that she has orchestrated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.180
entire Watcher horror story to get it&nbsp;
back, including paying off "John Graff"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.500
and "Pigtails" and manipulating the&nbsp;
psychologically fragile Andrew Pierce.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.460
This is quite a shock, as Theodora is&nbsp;
professionally connected with Detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:12.120
Chamberland. Part of Dean's theory is&nbsp;
that the cops are in on the conspiracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:17.580
covering up murders and worse. Of course,&nbsp;
these scenarios are unlikely, and the Watcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:21.840
could be someone we don't even meet over the&nbsp;
course of the series. After she passes away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.220
it's revealed that Theodora apparently&nbsp;
made up her story about being the Watcher.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:31.320
Theodora pretends to be the Watcher to give Dean&nbsp;
and herself some kind of closure. For herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.400
she needs it because she wants to solve&nbsp;
this last case before she dies. For Dean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.260
she sees the darkness and the obsession in him in&nbsp;
a way that Nora doesn't. Perhaps it's Theodora's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:44.402
own history with addiction that gives her&nbsp;
such insight into the other characters.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:47.700
And there you have it — your&nbsp;
explanation such as it is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.840
the ending of "The Watcher," Season 1. The&nbsp;
identity of the Watcher remains a mystery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.620
but whoever it is continues to haunt&nbsp;
the property, and may have competition.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.460
What would you do if you were being watched?&nbsp;
The show seems to suggest that you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:04.320
lose your entire mind, and maybe that's true.&nbsp;
The show essentially presents a situation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:09.720
uncomfortable horror and says there is no chance&nbsp;
of escape, then spends seven episodes proving it.

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:14.252
Maybe the act of being Watched is itself&nbsp;
some kind of crucible, a test of character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.252 --> 00:10:17.432
in which some of us might well&nbsp;
be proven to be of sterner stuff;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.432 --> 00:10:20.880
we just happen to be watching the story&nbsp;
of one family that couldn't handle it all.

